WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.700
Insulin never crosses the cell membrane, yet muscle cells pull in glucose within

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:13.940
minutes. How does a hormone outside wake up an enzyme drifting deep inside the cell?

3
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.900
The cell solves this problem by writing a temporary lipid address on its inner

4
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:20.900
membrane.

5
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:25.333
Amber insulin docks onto its receptor spanning the bilayer.

6
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:28.673
The receptor is a tyrosine kinase.

7
00:00:29.813 --> 00:00:35.833
It adds phosphates to its own tyrosines, then marks a nearby docking protein, IRS-1.

8
00:00:37.113 --> 00:00:41.913
Those fresh phosphotyrosines turn the docking protein into a busy landing pad.

9
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:46.247
Now PI 3-kinase arrives from the cytosol.

10
00:00:47.487 --> 00:00:52.747
Its regulatory subunit grips those phosphotyrosines using SH2 domains.

11
00:00:54.347 --> 00:00:59.007
Docking pulls the enzyme against the bilayer and loosens its regulatory brake.

12
00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:05.407
The catalytic subunit adds one phosphate at position 3 of the lipid's ring-shaped

13
00:01:05.407 --> 00:01:12.947
head. Membrane lipid PIP2, carrying two phosphates, becomes gold PIP3, carrying

14
00:01:12.947 --> 00:01:13.947
three.

15
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.930
That gold lipid patch is the membrane address for Akt.

16
00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:23.510
Before insulin arrives, Akt drifts folded and quiet in the cytosol.

17
00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:27.590
Its folded shape keeps its activation loop hidden.

18
00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:32.010
Akt carries a lipid-binding PH domain at one end.

19
00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.770
When the gold patch appears, that pocket grips it and anchors Akt.

20
00:01:37.783 --> 00:01:42.423
Binding the lipid also unfolds Akt, exposing its activation loop.

21
00:01:43.643 --> 00:01:49.043
A second kinase, PDK1, sits nearby on the lipid through its own PH domain.

22
00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:52.623
PDK1 phosphorylates that loop.

23
00:01:53.903 --> 00:01:57.983
Another kinase complex, mTORC2, marks Akt's tail.

24
00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:03.617
The loop phosphate does most of the work, and the tail phosphate adds full power.

25
00:02:04.917 --> 00:02:06.077
Keep this rule in mind.

26
00:02:07.637 --> 00:02:12.177
PIP3 recruits Akt to the membrane, where two phosphates activate it.

27
00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:16.517
Here, the upstream steps write that address when insulin arrives.

28
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.580
Let us test that rule with a drug.

29
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:25.120
Suppose a drug blocks the catalytic subunit of PI 3-kinase.

30
00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.160
Insulin still binds, and the receptor still marks IRS-1.

31
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.760
Will Akt still gain its activating phosphates?

32
00:02:39.633 --> 00:02:41.333
No, activation fails.

33
00:02:42.633 --> 00:02:46.893
The receptor and docking protein still light up, but no gold lipid appears.

34
00:02:48.233 --> 00:02:53.133
With no address, Akt stays folded in the cytosol, far from PDK1.

35
00:02:54.453 --> 00:02:56.553
Without the lipid, phosphorylation fails.

36
00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:02.780
Requiring a membrane lipid and two separate kinases works like a safety lock.

37
00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:09.180
Akt turns on mainly where active receptors build 3-phosphorylated lipids, and while

38
00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.200
those lipids last.

39
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:16.260
Put together, lipid docking and dual phosphorylation switch Akt on cleanly.
